1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,929
A CTO asks: "git status said 'up
to date' on the release laptop.

2
00:00:08,969 --> 00:00:12,840
We tagged, and the tag does
not contain yesterday's fix.

3
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,376
Why did Git say that?"

4
00:00:14,716 --> 00:00:19,874
Git said it because it was true about the
only thing git status looks at, which is a

5
00:00:19,914 --> 00:00:23,524
ref, a name that points at a
commit, stored on that laptop.

6
00:00:23,564 --> 00:00:28,659
git status never contacts a server. To see
why that sentence is not a technicality,

7
00:00:28,699 --> 00:00:32,186
you need to know what a remote
is. It's not a connection.

8
00:00:32,226 --> 00:00:37,012
It's a few lines of configuration and
some refs, all of them in your own .git.

9
00:00:37,052 --> 00:00:39,487
Keep that laptop in mind. It comes back.

10
00:00:41,327 --> 00:00:45,038
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:45,078 --> 00:00:49,772
This video opens the module on remote
operations. A repository is the dot git

12
00:00:49,812 --> 00:00:53,954
folder: the commits, the names that
point at them, and the settings.

13
00:00:53,994 --> 00:00:57,151
Until now every repository
in this course was alone.

14
00:00:57,191 --> 00:01:02,132
From here on there are at least two, and
the first skill is to keep them apart in

15
00:01:02,172 --> 00:01:02,747
your head.

16
00:01:03,087 --> 00:01:07,955
Two things today. First, look at what a
remote is inside .git: a name, a URL, a

17
00:01:07,995 --> 00:01:12,987
refspec, and the refs that refspec creates.
Second, take git clone apart into six

18
00:01:13,027 --> 00:01:18,206
steps and rebuild a clone by hand, so that
nothing about a fresh clone is a mystery.

19
00:01:18,246 --> 00:01:23,114
On the way you compare a normal clone, a
bare clone and a mirror clone by their

20
00:01:23,154 --> 00:01:27,960
refs, and you look at origin/HEAD, a
small ref that goes stale without telling

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:28,395
anyone.

22
00:01:28,735 --> 00:01:33,599
The project in this module is support-bot,
retrieval-augmented answers for a help

23
00:01:33,639 --> 00:01:38,381
desk. A bare repository on disk plays the
server. A bare repository on disk and

24
00:01:38,421 --> 00:01:42,740
GitHub differ in authentication and
in the rules a platform adds on top.

25
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:44,617
The Git mechanics are the same.

26
00:01:46,457 --> 00:01:48,035
Here's what you'll walk away with.

27
00:01:48,375 --> 00:01:49,787
After this video you can:

28
00:01:50,127 --> 00:01:54,100
Describe a remote as a name, URLs
and refspecs in the configuration.

29
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:57,116
Take git clone apart into
the commands it performs.

30
00:01:57,456 --> 00:02:00,868
Explain where origin/main is
stored and what origin/HEAD is.

31
00:02:01,208 --> 00:02:05,102
Compare a normal clone, a bare clone
and a mirror clone by their refs.

32
00:02:06,942 --> 00:02:11,493
Start with the model the whole module
rests on. There are at least three things

33
00:02:11,533 --> 00:02:12,190
called main.

34
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:17,024
Quick quiz. Which of these
commands contacts the server?

35
00:02:17,064 --> 00:02:21,233
A, git status. B, git log
origin/main. C, git fetch.

36
00:02:21,273 --> 00:02:22,205
Your answer?

37
00:02:27,045 --> 00:02:32,642
It's C, and only C. Git contacts another
repository only when you run a command that

38
00:02:32,682 --> 00:02:37,945
says so: clone, fetch, pull, push,
ls-remote, and a few git remote subcommands.

39
00:02:37,985 --> 00:02:43,381
Every other command, including status, log
origin/main and branch -vv, reads refs

40
00:02:43,421 --> 00:02:47,140
stored in your own .git. That
is the answer to the hook.

41
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:51,896
Now the definition. In one sentence: a
remote is a name in your repository's

42
00:02:51,936 --> 00:02:56,822
configuration that stands for another
repository: its URL, and the rules for copying

43
00:02:56,862 --> 00:02:59,109
refs between that repository and yours.

44
00:02:59,449 --> 00:03:04,831
Precisely: a remote is a configuration
section, [remote "<name>"], holding one or

45
00:03:04,871 --> 00:03:10,119
more URLs and one or more fetch refspecs.
A refspec has the form, optional plus

46
00:03:10,159 --> 00:03:15,675
sign, source, colon, destination, and it
maps ref names on one side to ref names on

47
00:03:15,715 --> 00:03:20,227
the other. The default written by
git clone and by git remote add is

48
00:03:20,267 --> 00:03:25,448
+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/<name>/*. Read
it aloud: every branch of the remote

49
00:03:25,488 --> 00:03:30,736
becomes a ref of yours under refs/remotes/<name>/,
and the plus sign allows the

50
00:03:30,776 --> 00:03:33,547
update even when it is not a fast-forward.

51
00:03:33,887 --> 00:03:38,851
The refs it creates are remote-tracking
branches. The glossary defines one as a ref

52
00:03:38,891 --> 00:03:42,167
that is used to follow changes
from another repository.

53
00:03:42,207 --> 00:03:46,990
The data-model manual is blunter: it is
how Git stores the last-known state of a

54
00:03:47,030 --> 00:03:51,813
branch in a remote repository. Last-known.
On screen is a fresh clone of today's

55
00:03:51,853 --> 00:03:56,214
project. main is your branch. origin/main
and origin/feature/reranker are

56
00:03:56,254 --> 00:04:00,857
remote-tracking branches. And origin is
only the name that git clone picks by

57
00:04:00,897 --> 00:04:01,339
default.

58
00:04:01,679 --> 00:04:07,721
Inside .git a remote occupies four places.
The [remote] and [branch] sections of

59
00:04:07,761 --> 00:04:12,587
.git/config. The refs under
refs/remotes/<name>/. Their reflogs.

60
00:04:12,627 --> 00:04:18,517
And FETCH_HEAD, rewritten by every fetch.
There is no object store per remote:

61
00:04:18,557 --> 00:04:22,927
objects from every remote land
in the one object database.

62
00:04:23,267 --> 00:04:28,757
Then git clone 🟢. In one sentence: it creates
a new repository, registers the source

63
00:04:28,797 --> 00:04:34,222
as the remote origin, fetches everything
the default refspec covers, and checks out

64
00:04:34,262 --> 00:04:38,041
one branch. Six steps, each of
which you can run yourself.

65
00:04:38,381 --> 00:04:41,638
Create the directory and an
empty repository: git init.

66
00:04:41,978 --> 00:04:46,655
Write the remote, its URL and the default
fetch refspec: git remote add origin

67
00:04:46,695 --> 00:04:47,018
<url>.

68
00:04:47,358 --> 00:04:52,153
Fetch: copy the objects, create a
remote-tracking branch for every branch of the

69
00:04:52,193 --> 00:04:53,482
remote, copy the tags.

70
00:04:53,822 --> 00:04:58,943
Record the remote's default branch as the
symbolic ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD.

71
00:04:59,283 --> 00:05:03,796
Create one local branch, named after the
branch that the remote's HEAD points at,

72
00:05:03,836 --> 00:05:07,281
starting at the same commit, with
that branch as its upstream.

73
00:05:07,621 --> 00:05:10,224
Check it out: fill the
index and the working tree.

74
00:05:10,564 --> 00:05:15,938
When is a clone not this? With --bare and
--mirror, which have no working tree and

75
00:05:15,978 --> 00:05:21,285
copy branches to refs/heads/* directly.
They differ from each other in one thing,

76
00:05:21,325 --> 00:05:26,368
the configuration, and that difference
decides whether a "backup" follows its

77
00:05:26,408 --> 00:05:28,283
source. You will see it fail.

78
00:05:30,123 --> 00:05:35,089
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
an entry in an address book with a filing

79
00:05:35,129 --> 00:05:40,217
instruction attached. "The supplier we call
origin is at this address; file our copy

80
00:05:40,257 --> 00:05:42,659
of whatever they call X under origin/X."

81
00:05:42,999 --> 00:05:48,115
It breaks in two places, and both matter.
The entry is not a connection: nothing is

82
00:05:48,155 --> 00:05:51,656
live, and the filed copies are
as old as your last visit.

83
00:05:51,696 --> 00:05:56,626
And the supplier is not special: it is a
full Git repository like yours, and the

84
00:05:56,666 --> 00:05:59,421
name origin exists only
in your address book.

85
00:05:59,761 --> 00:06:03,724
For clone, the analogy is moving
into a furnished copy of an office.

86
00:06:03,764 --> 00:06:08,433
You receive every document, a board that
shows where the head office's bookmarks

87
00:06:08,473 --> 00:06:13,260
stood this morning, and one desk set up
for work. On screen, the dashed labels are

88
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:18,027
that board, and main is your desk. It
breaks because nothing ties the copy to the

89
00:06:18,067 --> 00:06:20,794
original afterwards except
the address on file.

90
00:06:21,134 --> 00:06:25,490
So when you look at origin/main, say to
yourself: this is where main was on the

91
00:06:25,530 --> 00:06:27,882
server the last time
this repository asked.

92
00:06:29,722 --> 00:06:34,810
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper.
Draw two boxes, a server and your clone.

93
00:06:34,850 --> 00:06:39,280
The server has two branches, main
and feature/reranker, and one tag.

94
00:06:39,320 --> 00:06:42,698
Write down every ref your
clone has after git clone.

95
00:06:42,738 --> 00:06:46,248
Then say out loud how many
of them are local branches.

96
00:06:51,088 --> 00:06:55,929
Here's the answer. The server has two
branches. Your clone has two remote-tracking

97
00:06:55,969 --> 00:07:00,929
branches and exactly one local branch. The
server's feature/reranker is not a branch

98
00:07:00,969 --> 00:07:05,929
of yours. It's origin/feature/reranker, a
bookmark of where that branch was when you

99
00:07:05,969 --> 00:07:06,345
cloned.

100
00:07:08,185 --> 00:07:10,102
Part 1: what a clone wrote down.

101
00:07:10,442 --> 00:07:14,860
Predict which sections the
configuration has besides [core].

102
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:16,866
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

103
00:07:21,706 --> 00:07:27,486
Two. [remote "origin"] is the whole remote:
a URL, an absolute path here because the

104
00:07:27,526 --> 00:07:33,306
clone source was a local path, and one
refspec. [branch "main"] records the upstream

105
00:07:33,346 --> 00:07:36,631
of your branch main, which
a later video covers.

106
00:07:36,971 --> 00:07:39,100
Now compare the refs on the two sides.

107
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:44,467
The server: two branches and a tag. Your
clone: the tag under the same name, both

108
00:07:44,507 --> 00:07:49,722
branches under refs/remotes/origin/, one
local branch, and refs/remotes/origin/HEAD.

109
00:07:50,062 --> 00:07:55,049
git branch -a -vv is the quickest complete
view: local branches with their upstream

110
00:07:55,089 --> 00:07:57,896
in brackets, then the
remote-tracking branches.

111
00:07:58,236 --> 00:08:03,082
The server is a bare repository: no working
tree, and its directory is itself the

112
00:08:03,122 --> 00:08:03,927
Git directory.

113
00:08:04,267 --> 00:08:09,292
No index, and git status refuses. Notice
also what the listing lacks: logs/.

114
00:08:09,332 --> 00:08:14,691
core.logAllRefUpdates defaults to true in
a repository with a working tree and to

115
00:08:14,731 --> 00:08:20,090
false in a bare one, so a plain bare server
keeps no reflog, the list of values a

116
00:08:20,130 --> 00:08:25,688
ref has had. When a branch on such a server
is overwritten, the server has no record

117
00:08:25,728 --> 00:08:29,754
of the old value. The video on
forced pushes depends on this.

118
00:08:30,094 --> 00:08:36,456
A version note that the textbook attaches
here: Git 2.55 works inside any bare

119
00:08:36,496 --> 00:08:42,777
repository it finds, safe.bareRepository=all,
but the 2.55 manual states that

120
00:08:42,817 --> 00:08:48,276
explicit will be the default in Git
3.0. Under explicit, the git -C

121
00:08:48,316 --> 00:08:54,596
<bare-repository> form these demos use
fails, and git --git-dir=<path> works.

122
00:08:54,936 --> 00:08:58,977
One line of housekeeping. git remote
set-url 🟡 CAUTION rewrites the URL.

123
00:08:59,317 --> 00:09:05,137
The demos store a relative path so that
the commit IDs do not depend on where your

124
00:09:05,177 --> 00:09:11,140
lab root is. The last command, git config
get --all --show-names --regexp, needs Git

125
00:09:11,180 --> 00:09:16,857
2.46 or later. It prints the four lines
that are the entire relationship between

126
00:09:16,897 --> 00:09:18,715
your clone and the server.

127
00:09:19,055 --> 00:09:24,160
Part 2: a clone by hand.
labs/run ch12/clone-by-hand.

128
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:25,519
Steps 1 and 2.

129
00:09:25,859 --> 00:09:29,529
git remote add wrote the URL
and the default refspec.

130
00:09:29,569 --> 00:09:35,269
No network activity, no objects. Step 3.
Predict: after git fetch origin, how many

131
00:09:35,309 --> 00:09:36,739
local branches exist?

132
00:09:41,579 --> 00:09:46,913
None. If you said one, you're in good
company. The fetch created remote-tracking

133
00:09:46,953 --> 00:09:52,555
branches, a tag and origin/HEAD, and nothing
else. git status still says "No commits

134
00:09:52,595 --> 00:09:56,720
yet": HEAD names a main that has
not been born. Steps 5 and 6:

135
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:08,187
git switch main found no local main, found
exactly one remote-tracking branch of

136
00:10:08,227 --> 00:10:13,290
that name, and created the branch from
origin/main with upstream configuration.

137
00:10:13,330 --> 00:10:17,618
That's the picture of a fresh clone
again, this time built by hand.

138
00:10:17,658 --> 00:10:21,364
This is the --guess behavior
of git switch, on by default.

139
00:10:21,404 --> 00:10:23,948
The older spelling is git checkout main.

140
00:10:24,288 --> 00:10:28,655
A real git clone differs from the
hand-built version in three details.

141
00:10:28,695 --> 00:10:33,503
It takes the name of the initial branch
from the remote's HEAD, not from your

142
00:10:33,543 --> 00:10:37,595
init.defaultBranch. It writes
clone: from <url> into the reflogs.

143
00:10:37,635 --> 00:10:42,695
And when the source is a local path it
does not transfer a pack: the object files

144
00:10:42,735 --> 00:10:44,332
are hard-linked or copied.

145
00:10:44,672 --> 00:10:50,877
Part 3: options that change the
result. labs/run ch12/clone-options.

146
00:10:51,217 --> 00:10:56,701
--origin company changes the name of the
remote and with it the prefix of every

147
00:10:56,741 --> 00:11:02,435
remote-tracking branch. --branch release/0.1
changes which local branch is created

148
00:11:02,475 --> 00:11:08,170
and checked out. It does not limit what is
fetched, and company/HEAD still records

149
00:11:08,210 --> 00:11:10,338
the remote's own default, main.

150
00:11:10,677 --> 00:11:13,114
--branch also accepts a tag.

151
00:11:13,454 --> 00:11:18,159
The result is a detached HEAD at the tagged
commit: HEAD holds a commit ID instead

152
00:11:18,199 --> 00:11:22,788
of a branch name. The warning only says
that the name given was an annotated tag

153
00:11:22,828 --> 00:11:24,176
object and not a commit.

154
00:11:24,516 --> 00:11:30,220
--single-branch narrows the refspec to
one branch. Every later git fetch in that

155
00:11:30,260 --> 00:11:36,034
clone follows only that branch. --depth
implies it, and CI systems produce clones

156
00:11:36,074 --> 00:11:36,752
like this.

157
00:11:37,092 --> 00:11:40,181
Now bare and mirror. Predict
the difference in their refs.

158
00:11:50,821 --> 00:11:56,396
The refs are identical. If you looked
for the difference there, so does almost

159
00:11:56,436 --> 00:12:01,434
everyone. Both are bare, and both
copied the branches to refs/heads/*.

160
00:12:01,474 --> 00:12:07,120
The difference is in the configuration.
The --bare clone has a URL and no fetch

161
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:12,950
refspec. The --mirror clone has the refspec
+refs/*:refs/*, every ref, same name,

162
00:12:12,990 --> 00:12:14,822
forced, and mirror = true.

163
00:12:15,162 --> 00:12:19,089
Part 4: the consequence.
labs/run ch12/mirror-refresh.

164
00:12:19,129 --> 00:12:23,644
The server has moved: one new
commit on main and a new branch.

165
00:12:23,984 --> 00:12:29,311
With no refspec, git fetch downloads the
remote's HEAD into FETCH_HEAD and moves no

166
00:12:29,351 --> 00:12:34,289
ref. This "backup" still has the old
main and has never heard of release/1.0.

167
00:12:34,629 --> 00:12:35,587
The mirror follows.

168
00:12:35,927 --> 00:12:39,181
And with --prune it deletes
what the source deleted.

169
00:12:39,221 --> 00:12:41,145
That faithfulness is the catch.

170
00:12:41,485 --> 00:12:46,325
A mirror reproduces a destructive force
push or a branch deletion at its next

171
00:12:46,365 --> 00:12:51,396
update, and being bare it keeps no reflog.
It is a replica of the present, not a

172
00:12:51,436 --> 00:12:52,600
record of the past.

173
00:12:52,940 --> 00:12:57,849
Part 5: origin/HEAD.
labs/run ch12/origin-head.

174
00:12:58,189 --> 00:13:02,408
A symbolic ref, which is a ref that
holds the name of another ref.

175
00:13:02,448 --> 00:13:05,699
In a files-format repository,
it's a one-line file.

176
00:13:05,739 --> 00:13:10,990
It makes the bare remote name a valid
revision, which is why git log origin works.

177
00:13:11,030 --> 00:13:16,217
It's a local record, and it goes stale.
The server's default branch is now trunk.

178
00:13:16,257 --> 00:13:18,992
Predict what git fetch
does to origin/HEAD.

179
00:13:23,832 --> 00:13:28,637
Nothing. The fetch brought the new
branch and left origin/HEAD on main.

180
00:13:28,677 --> 00:13:33,278
git remote set-head origin --auto
🟡 asks the server and repoints it.

181
00:13:33,318 --> 00:13:38,738
If the old default branch is later deleted
and pruned, the symbolic ref dangles:

182
00:13:39,078 --> 00:13:41,956
And a fetch creates origin/HEAD
when it is missing.

183
00:13:42,296 --> 00:13:47,796
Look at the command that created it: on
Git 2.55.0, git fetch --dry-run wrote the

184
00:13:47,836 --> 00:13:53,267
ref. "Dry run" does not cover this side
effect. The textbook's version note: git

185
00:13:53,307 --> 00:13:59,013
fetch has created the ref when missing since
Git 2.48, and what it does when the ref

186
00:13:59,053 --> 00:14:04,758
exists and disagrees is the per-remote
setting remote.<name>.followRemoteHEAD, whose

187
00:14:04,798 --> 00:14:10,025
default, create, never touches an existing
ref. The recommendation is warn in

188
00:14:10,065 --> 00:14:11,256
long-lived clones.

189
00:14:13,096 --> 00:14:14,744
Five mistakes to watch for.

190
00:14:15,084 --> 00:14:18,439
Reading git status as a
statement about the server.

191
00:14:18,479 --> 00:14:23,965
Root cause: status reads refs/remotes/origin/main
in your own .git, which is as old

192
00:14:24,005 --> 00:14:25,230
as your last fetch.

193
00:14:25,570 --> 00:14:29,853
Using git clone --bare plus a
nightly git fetch as a backup.

194
00:14:29,893 --> 00:14:35,328
Root cause: a bare clone has a URL and
no fetch refspec, so the fetch writes

195
00:14:35,368 --> 00:14:37,346
FETCH_HEAD and moves no ref.

196
00:14:37,686 --> 00:14:42,795
Treating a mirror as a backup. Root cause:
a mirror copies every ref, forced, and

197
00:14:42,835 --> 00:14:48,135
being bare keeps no reflog, so it reproduces
a destructive push or a deletion at its

198
00:14:48,175 --> 00:14:48,898
next update.

199
00:14:49,238 --> 00:14:53,026
Scripts that trust origin/HEAD
after a default-branch rename.

200
00:14:53,066 --> 00:14:57,858
Root cause: origin/HEAD is a local
symbolic ref that a fetch with the default

201
00:14:57,898 --> 00:15:01,874
setting does not update; git remote
set-head origin --auto does.

202
00:15:02,214 --> 00:15:06,234
Two people saying "origin" and
meaning different repositories.

203
00:15:06,274 --> 00:15:11,669
Root cause: the name exists only in each
clone's configuration; git remote -v shows

204
00:15:11,709 --> 00:15:12,914
what it stands for.

205
00:15:14,754 --> 00:15:19,557
Now, out of the lab. A nightly
evaluation job clones with --depth 1.

206
00:15:19,597 --> 00:15:25,540
An engineer debugging in that workspace
types git switch release/2.3 and gets fatal:

207
00:15:25,580 --> 00:15:29,813
invalid reference, although the
branch exists on the server.

208
00:15:29,853 --> 00:15:35,582
The clone's refspec does not include it:
--depth implies --single-branch, and the

209
00:15:35,622 --> 00:15:37,363
refspec names one branch.

210
00:15:37,703 --> 00:15:42,556
